/* book: lead: A microwave resonator stores electric and magnetic energy and returns it at one frequency, f0. Energy sloshes between a capacitor and an inductor, or in the fields of a cavity or line section. Q, the quality factor, compares energy stored with energy lost per radian. High Q gives a narrow response; low Q gives a broad one. Resonators sit inside filters, oscillators, duplexers and frequency meters  */
/* book Figure 1.8.1: The resonator of worked example 1: QL = 100 gives a 20 MHz band at 2 GHz. Loosened to critical coupling (Qe = Q0 = 500) the same resonator has QL = 250 and an 8 MHz band. */
